A forest in drake, and the fact-resolution finding it rests on

Two things, and the second is why the first is shaped as it is.

THE FINDING: co-declaration drops the fact

`SubtypeFactVisibilityTest` inserts one `FixReport` (`FixReport <: Marine <: World`, and `<: JSON`)
and asks which declared types see it:

    SOLO — one knowledge, one rule
        World      FIRED        Marine     FIRED
        FixReport  FIRED        JSON       FIRED
    COMBINED — all four declared together
        <none fired>

Inheritance IS honoured: a rule declared two levels up sees the fact. But declare two types from one
hierarchy in a single knowledge and the resolver picks neither —

    DefaultTypeResolver   Unable to resolve type '…$$anon$1' due to ambiguity.
    AbstractRuleSessionOps  Can not map type for '…', insert operation skipped.

— and the insert is SKIPPED. The fact never reaches working memory, every rule goes quiet, nothing
throws, and the only trace is a `java.util.logging` warning. Recorded in DRACO.md's gotchas because
no test would fail when it happens.

The corpus is safe today by shape rather than by design: each medium's session declares SIBLINGS
(`PositionReport` + `FlightIntent`), and draco's validation session a deliberately disjoint taxonomy
(`Problem` / `TypeDefinition` / `DomainType`). Nothing declares an ancestor beside a descendant.

This also corrects what GitHub #63 says the actor dispatch is FOR. Not routing — the engine routes
siblings correctly on its own. **The dispatch converts hierarchy into siblings**: a message arrives
at whatever height its type sits, and the dispatch emits a fact at a fixed flat level, so the one
thing the engine cannot do safely is never asked of it.

THE SCENARIO: can a message domain be said in today's drake?

draco has no `message domain` and no `transform domain` construct. The question is whether they can
be built DE FACTO from what drake already has, and the answer is yes — 22 definitions,
0 rejected, 0 drifted, and every parsed shape correct:

    forest/     Forest (root super-domain), AshBirchAlarm (the transform rule)
    ash/        Ash, AshSap, AlarmSignal, DroughtCue, Infochemical,
                AshJasmonate, AshAbscisate, Micromolar, Compound, RootInterface
    birch/      Birch, BirchSap, AlarmSignal, Infochemical,
                BirchJasmonate, Micromolar, Compound
    ash/birch/  Ash_Birch, Potency, Marker

Four constructs with no precedent in either corpus all held: `super` (carried by ZERO definitions
before this — `scenario.forest.Forest` on all three domains), a transform domain whose package
deliberately breaks the package/type-name coupling (`domain scenario ash birch Ash_Birch`), a
cross-package derivation into a target domain (`Potency from scenario birch Micromolar`), and an
underscore in a type name.

The subject matter is load-bearing, not decoration. Species-specific metabolic fingerprints are why
`Ash.Micromolar` is not `Birch.Micromolar`, and the fungal network's standardizing of diverse tree
inputs IS the leaf conversion — `Ash_Birch.Potency` derives Birch's primal and takes Ash's as its
factory argument, attenuating on the crossing. The transform domain declares one type per leaf
conversion and nothing else; every composite is a factory application, which drake's application
surface already writes. `Infochemical` is simultaneously the dispatch tag and the protection
boundary, which in the biology is one membrane: what may cross the root interface into the fungus,
while the rest of the sap stays inside the tree.

WHAT IT DOES NOT PROVE, stated plainly

Parsing is the weaker half. The scenario has never been generated or compiled, and the same four
unprecedented constructs have not been through the Generator. `RootInterface`'s dispatch parsed
exactly as predicted — a Scala `match` carried as host-opaque text, single-line because a value must
be. That it parses proves nothing: drake can store the text, cannot read it, and cannot project it
to any other target. That is the gap #63 exists to close, now with a corpus to close it against.

No `src/main` or `src/mods` changed. The suite runs as a formality rather than a gate.

